{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2072 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "a8032022325f6afa1c7b7e2a42d819d239f1e87bcb1fe57c58f3a2c644b5a9ad" :edb/db-before-t 2071 :edb/db-after-t 2072 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535384 50 #inst "2026-10-08T23:13:18.640Z" 13194139535384 true] [17592186171742 1145 "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^2 = 4p(x-h)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $y$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} - 8x - 6y + 33 &= 0 \\\\\ny^{2} - 6y &= 8x - 33 \\\\\n(y - 3)^{2} - 9 &= 8x - 33 \\\\\n(y - 3)^{2} &= 8x - 33 + 9 \\\\\n(y - 3)^{2} &= 8x - 24 \\\\\n(y - 3)^{2} &= 8(x - 3) \\\\\n(y - 3)^{2} &= 4(2)(x - 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (3,3)\n$$" 13194139535384 false] [17592186171742 1145 "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^2 = 4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $y$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-8x-6y+33 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-6y & =8x-33 \\\\ (y-3)^{2}-9 & =8x-33 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =8x-33+9 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =8x-24 \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =8(x-3) \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =4(2)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k) = (3,3).$" 13194139535384 true] [17592186171755 1100 "Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $5x^2 -10x - 4y - 7 = 0$." 13194139535384 false] [17592186171755 1100 "Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $5x^2 -10x - 4y - 7 = 0.$" 13194139535384 true] [17592186171755 1145 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^2 = 4p(y-k)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $x$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x^{2} - 10x - 4y - 7 &= 0 \\\\\n5x^{2} - 10x &= 4y + 7 \\\\\n5[x^{2} - 2x] &= 4y + 7 \\\\\n5[(x - 1)^{2} - 1] &= 4y + 7 \\\\\n5(x - 1)^{2} - 5 &= 4y + 7 \\\\\n5(x - 1)^{2} &= 4y + 7 + 5 \\\\\n5(x - 1)^{2} &= 4y + 12 \\\\\n5(x - 1)^{2} &= 4(y + 3) \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} &= \\frac{4}{5}(y + 3) \\\\\n(x - 1)^{2} &= 4(\\frac{1}{5})(y + 3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are\n\n$$\n(h,k) = (1,-3)\n$$" 13194139535384 false] [17592186171755 1145 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^2 = 4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $x$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-10x-4y-7 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-10x & =4y+7 \\\\ 5[{x}^{2}-2x] & =4y+7 \\\\ 5[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =4y+7 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2}-5 & =4y+7 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2} & =4y+7+5 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2} & =4y+12 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2} & =4(y+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{5}(y+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{5})(y+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k) = (1,-3).$" 13194139535384 true] [17592186171890 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346897 13194139535384 true] [17592186171890 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346904 13194139535384 true] [17592186171890 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346911 13194139535384 true] [17592186171890 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346918 13194139535384 true] [17592186171890 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346946 13194139535384 true] [17592186171951 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346890 13194139535384 true] [17592186171951 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346925 13194139535384 true] [17592186171951 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346932 13194139535384 true] [17592186171951 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346939 13194139535384 true] [17592186172206 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535384 true] [17592186172206 1100 "Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $3x^{2} + 6x + 4y + 15 = 0$." 13194139535384 false] [17592186172206 1100 "Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $3{x}^{2}+6x+4y+15=0.$" 13194139535384 true] [17592186172206 1145 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $x$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+6x+4y+15 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+6x & =-4y-15 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+2x] & =-4y-15 \\\\ 3[(x+1)^{2}-1] & =-4y-15 \\\\ 3(x+1)^{2}-3 & =-4y-15 \\\\ 3(x+1)^{2} & =-4y-15+3 \\\\ 3(x+1)^{2} & =-4y-12 \\\\ 3(x+1)^{2} & =-4(y+3) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}(y+3) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})(y+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k)=(-1,-3).$" 13194139535384 true] [17592186346890 1017 #edb/ref 17592186346891 13194139535384 true] [17592186346890 1089 "q-103668" 13194139535384 true] [17592186346890 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535384 true] [17592186346890 1100 "Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola ${x}^{2}+2x-2y-1=0.$" 13194139535384 true] [17592186346890 1113 #uuid "6b83c7c7-5cc8-50a8-88f2-06b2797824da" 13194139535384 true] [17592186346890 1145 "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $x$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-2y-1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =2y+1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}-1 & =2y+1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =2y+1+1 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =2y+2 \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})(y+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k)=(-1,-1).$" 13194139535384 true] [17592186346891 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346892 1319413953